## Solution

**Setup.** The tank is 1 m high. At height $h$ above the bottom ($0 \le h \le 1$), the horizontal cross-section is the finite region bounded by $y = x^2$ and $y = 2 - h - 3x^2$, and the density is $\rho(h) = 1000\sqrt{2-h}$ kg/m³.

**Cross-sectional area.** The curves meet when $x^2 = 2 - h - 3x^2$, i.e. $4x^2 = 2-h$, giving $x = \pm \tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{2-h}$. The upper curve is $y = 2-h-3x^2$ and the lower is $y = x^2$, so the vertical gap is $(2-h-3x^2) - x^2 = 2 - h - 4x^2$. With $a = \tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{2-h}$:

$$A(h) = \int_{-a}^{a}(2-h-4x^2)\,dx = 2\Big[(2-h)x - \tfrac{4}{3}x^3\Big]_0^a = 2\Big[\tfrac{1}{2}(2-h)^{3/2} - \tfrac{1}{6}(2-h)^{3/2}\Big] = \tfrac{2}{3}(2-h)^{3/2}.$$

**Mass of a thin layer.** A layer of thickness $dh$ at height $h$ has volume $A(h)\,dh$ and mass

$$dm = \rho(h)\,A(h)\,dh = 1000\sqrt{2-h}\cdot\tfrac{2}{3}(2-h)^{3/2}\,dh = \tfrac{2000}{3}(2-h)^2\,dh.$$

**Work.** Each layer must be lifted from height $h$ to the top of the tank (height $1$), a distance $1-h$. With $g = 9.8$:

$$W = \int_0^1 \frac{2000}{3}(2-h)^2 \cdot 9.8 \cdot (1-h)\,dh = \frac{19600}{3}\int_0^1 (2-h)^2(1-h)\,dh.$$

**Evaluating the integral.** Expanding $(2-h)^2(1-h) = (4-4h+h^2)(1-h) = 4 - 8h + 5h^2 - h^3$:

$$\int_0^1 (4 - 8h + 5h^2 - h^3)\,dh = \Big[4h - 4h^2 + \tfrac{5}{3}h^3 - \tfrac{1}{4}h^4\Big]_0^1 = 4 - 4 + \tfrac{5}{3} - \tfrac{1}{4} = \frac{20-3}{12} = \frac{17}{12}.$$

**Final result.**

$$W = \frac{19600}{3}\cdot\frac{17}{12} = \frac{19600\cdot 17}{36} = \frac{333200}{36} = \boxed{\dfrac{83300}{9}\text{ joules}} \approx 9255.6\text{ J}.$$

